WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.940
- With an Oscar and two Golden Globes,

00:00:01.940 --> 00:00:04.310
she's one of the best
actresses of her generation,

00:00:04.310 --> 00:00:06.950
she's one of my personal
favorites, she's also an activist

00:00:06.950 --> 00:00:08.630
and now a children's book author,

00:00:08.630 --> 00:00:11.330
her new book is called
"Natalie Portman's Fables."

00:00:11.330 --> 00:00:12.300
It's pretty rad.

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:14.260
I literally checked it out recently.

00:00:14.260 --> 00:00:15.350
You should check it out as well.

00:00:15.350 --> 00:00:16.680
It's great, your kids will love it.

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:18.997
Let's say hi to Natalie Portman.

00:00:20.627 --> 00:00:22.249
- Hi.

00:00:22.249 --> 00:00:25.770
- Hi, so, first of all,
I am such a huge fan

00:00:25.770 --> 00:00:27.880
this is like these are days that my job,

00:00:27.880 --> 00:00:29.550
this new job is so awesome

00:00:29.550 --> 00:00:31.860
'cause never in the world
would I ever meet you.

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:34.970
So well where are you and
what are you up to there?

00:00:34.970 --> 00:00:37.460
- Well, I'm in Sydney,
Australia right now.

00:00:37.460 --> 00:00:41.081
I'm getting ready to film
Thor, the new Thor and--

00:00:41.081 --> 00:00:45.770
- Yes, it's so cool that
you're going to be Lady Thor.

00:00:45.770 --> 00:00:47.900
So do you get all of Thor's powers?

00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:51.850
Is my real question like
I'm a huge nerd for Avengers

00:00:51.850 --> 00:00:54.690
any kind of super hero,
like I've seen them all.

00:00:54.690 --> 00:00:55.990
So do you get powers?

00:00:55.990 --> 00:00:57.113
Is my question.

00:00:58.020 --> 00:01:01.600
- So she does have powers.

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:03.810
It's not exactly the same as Thor

00:01:03.810 --> 00:01:08.810
it's her own version of it and
she's called the Mighty Thor.

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:11.540
- Oh my gosh!

00:01:11.540 --> 00:01:13.620
What a cool character to play.

00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:16.280
So who is your personal favorite superhero

00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:17.860
or favorite superpower?

00:01:17.860 --> 00:01:22.210
- Well I have to say Thor I think

00:01:22.210 --> 00:01:25.770
as the greatest superhero
but of course I mean,

00:01:25.770 --> 00:01:30.770
I'm so excited that we're
gonna have a Black Widow movie

00:01:31.650 --> 00:01:33.698
and Captain Marvel of course

00:01:33.698 --> 00:01:36.380
and all the women of Black Panther and

00:01:37.330 --> 00:01:41.050
there's so many exciting
characters coming up.

00:01:41.050 --> 00:01:42.340
- I know I think it's really important

00:01:42.340 --> 00:01:44.010
what you just said too
because I don't know

00:01:44.010 --> 00:01:46.400
I feel like when we were kids

00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:49.100
there wasn't as much
representation for girls.

00:01:49.100 --> 00:01:50.290
It's kinda cool 'cause

00:01:50.290 --> 00:01:52.480
not only my little boy has
all these like boy heroes

00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:54.060
to look up to but also female ones.

00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:56.630
So you're right in that
sense it's very cool.

00:01:56.630 --> 00:01:57.660
- Yeah, that's totally true.

00:01:57.660 --> 00:01:59.670
I mean, I think people usually say

00:01:59.670 --> 00:02:00.950
how great it is for the girls

00:02:00.950 --> 00:02:02.930
but it's really important for the boys.

00:02:02.930 --> 00:02:05.440
For the boys to get to see women

00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:06.930
in those kinds of characters

00:02:06.930 --> 00:02:10.540
and in those kinds of personalities and

00:02:10.540 --> 00:02:14.060
it just expands opportunity
for how we all see each other.

00:02:14.060 --> 00:02:17.320
- Yeah, that's an amazing point
of view perspective as well.

00:02:17.320 --> 00:02:19.740
So what have you learned about yourself

00:02:19.740 --> 00:02:21.720
during this strange time of separation?

00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:25.130
And it's just been a weird 2020 for people

00:02:25.130 --> 00:02:26.390
and everybody's kinda got their own thing

00:02:26.390 --> 00:02:28.580
they've been going through,
so what have you learned?

00:02:28.580 --> 00:02:33.580
- Yeah, I've been so amazed
by everyone's resilience

00:02:34.070 --> 00:02:38.453
and flexibility and ability
to adapt and community.

00:02:39.320 --> 00:02:42.100
It really has been

00:02:42.100 --> 00:02:46.410
such a devastating time
obviously for so many people

00:02:46.410 --> 00:02:51.410
with lives lost there's
also been beautiful sides

00:02:51.930 --> 00:02:56.930
of seeing how much time
spent with your family

00:02:58.240 --> 00:02:59.740
how meaningful that is.

00:02:59.740 --> 00:03:02.210
Oh, it definitely makes you
cherish your relationships

00:03:02.210 --> 00:03:03.730
with loved ones more.

00:03:03.730 --> 00:03:05.200
- Yeah Amen.

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:06.670
Have you been cooking for your family?

00:03:06.670 --> 00:03:07.503
Do you cook a lot?

00:03:07.503 --> 00:03:10.814
I'm not a great cook but I can.

00:03:10.814 --> 00:03:11.880
(giggles)

00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:13.150
- I love cooking.

00:03:13.150 --> 00:03:17.090
I'm not like I have no training or skills

00:03:17.090 --> 00:03:20.160
like if anyone saw the way I
chop my vegetables or whatever,

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:22.760
they'd be horrified, but I love it

00:03:22.760 --> 00:03:26.580
and I love trying new things and yeah

00:03:26.580 --> 00:03:28.920
it's been a fun period of experimentation

00:03:28.920 --> 00:03:30.030
'cause there were so many things

00:03:30.030 --> 00:03:32.890
that we used to like order
in or go to restaurants

00:03:32.890 --> 00:03:34.300
that my kids loved

00:03:34.300 --> 00:03:36.370
but all of a sudden we
couldn't get any more

00:03:36.370 --> 00:03:38.550
and I had to figure out
how to make it at home.

00:03:38.550 --> 00:03:40.080
- Oh my God, you're such a nice mom!

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:42.304
I'm just like, well, it's not happening.

00:03:42.304 --> 00:03:43.610
(laughs)

00:03:43.610 --> 00:03:45.530
Dream a different dream.

00:03:45.530 --> 00:03:46.830
Well, what's your go-to dish?

00:03:46.830 --> 00:03:48.520
Like if you're gonna
like whip something up

00:03:48.520 --> 00:03:50.120
for like your kids or something.

00:03:51.330 --> 00:03:54.510
- I like making like a vegetable couscous

00:03:54.510 --> 00:03:59.510
I use like kid friendly
vegetables that I know they'll eat

00:04:00.410 --> 00:04:04.550
but couscous takes five minutes
to cook the actual grain

00:04:04.550 --> 00:04:07.770
and then the vegetables also is like

00:04:07.770 --> 00:04:09.780
you kind of like cut everything
up and throw it in a pot

00:04:09.780 --> 00:04:13.030
and then it's, you can
wait for it to be done

00:04:13.030 --> 00:04:14.610
do other stuff.

00:04:14.610 --> 00:04:17.180
I always find those the easiest for kids.

00:04:17.180 --> 00:04:19.420
Half the time you're like
holding one kid in your arm

00:04:19.420 --> 00:04:21.360
while you're throwing
stuff in the pot so, and--

00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:24.675
- Or they're yelling at each
other and you're like, oh gosh.

00:04:24.675 --> 00:04:26.540
(laughs)

00:04:26.540 --> 00:04:28.250
Well, kids are so honest.

00:04:28.250 --> 00:04:31.070
So what's one thing that came
out of their mouths recently

00:04:31.070 --> 00:04:32.143
that surprised you?

00:04:33.075 --> 00:04:35.730
(laughs)

00:04:35.730 --> 00:04:40.010
- So my son got to go
to camp here actually

00:04:40.010 --> 00:04:42.110
and he said to me

00:04:43.197 --> 00:04:46.660
"mom some girls at camp said
you look good for your age."

00:04:46.660 --> 00:04:47.493
- Oh!

00:04:50.345 --> 00:04:53.544
- Then he said, "it's good
you keep your sunglasses on

00:04:53.544 --> 00:04:56.120
because it covers your wrinkles."

00:04:56.120 --> 00:04:57.538
So I was like thank you.

00:04:57.538 --> 00:04:59.920
- Oh my gosh!

00:04:59.920 --> 00:05:02.057
That's amazing, my kids--

00:05:02.057 --> 00:05:04.620
- And then he said I love
you so it makes up for it.

00:05:04.620 --> 00:05:08.630
- I know, my kids will walk up
to, they think big as adult.

00:05:08.630 --> 00:05:11.330
Like when they say big
they just mean adult

00:05:12.270 --> 00:05:15.660
but they'll walk up to people
and be like, "you're so big."

00:05:15.660 --> 00:05:18.410
And like that's not really
what everyone wants to hear.

00:05:19.480 --> 00:05:21.820
But they for some reason they think

00:05:21.820 --> 00:05:23.890
no 'cause you're a big I'm little person

00:05:23.890 --> 00:05:25.320
you're a big person they think yeah

00:05:25.320 --> 00:05:28.333
it's really, really
fun to explain Natalie.

00:05:29.860 --> 00:05:32.500
Well, as a girl, what book
series were you obsessed with?

00:05:32.500 --> 00:05:33.370
'Cause now you're an author

00:05:33.370 --> 00:05:35.450
so what really inspired you as a kid?

00:05:35.450 --> 00:05:39.270
- Oh, I mean this is an older kid

00:05:39.270 --> 00:05:40.860
than like my kids are right now

00:05:40.860 --> 00:05:43.810
but when I was in like nine to 12,

00:05:43.810 --> 00:05:46.810
I was obsessed with
"The Baby-Sitters Club."

00:05:46.810 --> 00:05:47.643
- Oh my gosh, yes!

00:05:47.643 --> 00:05:50.770
- Like I used to wait for
the next one to come out

00:05:50.770 --> 00:05:52.890
and like line up at the bookstore

00:05:52.890 --> 00:05:56.180
and read it immediately in
the car on the way home.

00:05:56.180 --> 00:05:59.320
And I was just like, it was my life.

00:05:59.320 --> 00:06:01.787
- Then now they have the Netflix
series that just came out,

00:06:01.787 --> 00:06:02.850
"The Baby-Sitters Club" yeah.

00:06:02.850 --> 00:06:03.683
- It's so good.

00:06:03.683 --> 00:06:05.100
- I know 'cause my six year old

00:06:05.100 --> 00:06:07.220
should not be watching
that but I found her

00:06:07.220 --> 00:06:08.490
I mean it's just kind of a weird thing

00:06:08.490 --> 00:06:11.910
for a six year old to watch I
guess but she loves it but--

00:06:11.910 --> 00:06:13.550
- But I think a lot of it would probably

00:06:13.550 --> 00:06:15.930
just go over their heads it's
nothing like nothing wild.

00:06:15.930 --> 00:06:17.740
- Exactly, she doesn't even understand.

00:06:17.740 --> 00:06:20.240
The only thing she does
understand is the kissing parts

00:06:20.240 --> 00:06:23.140
in any movie or anything
she's like "oh my God!"

00:06:23.140 --> 00:06:25.380
She like totally freaks out

00:06:25.380 --> 00:06:27.620
and she can't help but
like smile from ear to ear.

00:06:27.620 --> 00:06:30.805
I'm so much in trouble
when she's a teenager.

00:06:30.805 --> 00:06:32.900
- It's so funny, it's so funny.

00:06:32.900 --> 00:06:37.470
They just really like
glom onto that so early.

00:06:37.470 --> 00:06:39.200
- Well we both are in children's books

00:06:39.200 --> 00:06:41.430
so what was your writing process?

00:06:41.430 --> 00:06:44.660
- We love your children's
book we read it all the time.

00:06:44.660 --> 00:06:46.693
- Oh, thank you, thank you so much.

00:06:48.280 --> 00:06:50.150
- I have a three and a
half year old little girl

00:06:50.150 --> 00:06:54.820
and she like, I made up my
own tune with the song and--

00:06:54.820 --> 00:06:57.290
- Oh my gosh, that's so nice!

00:06:57.290 --> 00:06:59.170
Well, my kids are gonna flip I literally

00:06:59.170 --> 00:07:01.280
like was reading this during lunch and I,

00:07:01.280 --> 00:07:03.340
this is such a great idea.

00:07:03.340 --> 00:07:05.210
Where did you come up with the idea?

00:07:05.210 --> 00:07:08.760
- So when I was reading
books to my kids I realized,

00:07:08.760 --> 00:07:12.680
so my son is nine and my
little girl is three and a half

00:07:12.680 --> 00:07:17.180
and the books that we got
when she was born as presents

00:07:17.180 --> 00:07:20.800
were so different than the
ones we had gotten for my son.

00:07:20.800 --> 00:07:25.800
And I realized that that
kind of like normal books

00:07:26.810 --> 00:07:31.450
aren't addressing like gender issues,

00:07:31.450 --> 00:07:35.420
the books that boys get
too and that the books that

00:07:35.420 --> 00:07:37.840
we were reading him the kind of classics

00:07:37.840 --> 00:07:41.130
had almost all male characters.

00:07:41.130 --> 00:07:44.640
And so I thought what if
you took the classic stories

00:07:44.640 --> 00:07:47.747
like the "Tortoise and the
Hare," "The Three Little Pigs,"

00:07:47.747 --> 00:07:49.160
"The Country Mouse and the City Mouse"

00:07:49.160 --> 00:07:51.430
which are the three stories I retold

00:07:51.430 --> 00:07:54.840
and just made it equal genders

00:07:54.840 --> 00:07:58.070
just so that there's boy
characters and girl characters.

00:07:58.070 --> 00:07:59.670
So that boys and girls

00:07:59.670 --> 00:08:02.580
when they're reading these
classic stories are exposed

00:08:02.580 --> 00:08:07.120
to girl feelings and boy feelings alike.

00:08:07.120 --> 00:08:09.880
And because it's really timmy stories

00:08:09.880 --> 00:08:12.020
are the way that we learn empathy.

00:08:12.020 --> 00:08:15.310
We learn to imagine how
someone else might be feeling

00:08:15.310 --> 00:08:17.950
or what someone else's day is like.

00:08:17.950 --> 00:08:19.967
Because when you read a book
you care about the characters

00:08:19.967 --> 00:08:22.440
and when you watch a movie
you care about the characters.

00:08:22.440 --> 00:08:26.130
And so you're basically practicing
caring for somebody else

00:08:26.130 --> 00:08:28.230
and what their life is like.

00:08:28.230 --> 00:08:32.790
So to me, it was so important
to teach both my kids empathy

00:08:32.790 --> 00:08:35.480
for all people and all animals

00:08:35.480 --> 00:08:39.400
'cause all the characters
in the stories are animals

00:08:39.400 --> 00:08:43.080
which also made me, it was
a big effect on me as a kid

00:08:43.080 --> 00:08:46.430
like reading books about animals
with feelings and thoughts

00:08:46.430 --> 00:08:48.290
and who talked to each other.

00:08:48.290 --> 00:08:50.650
I mean, I definitely
know that had an impact

00:08:50.650 --> 00:08:53.250
on me becoming vegetarian
at a young age too

00:08:53.250 --> 00:08:58.170
because it would be hurt
if I wanted to eat them.

00:08:58.170 --> 00:09:00.710
- Yeah, like I don't know what to say.

00:09:00.710 --> 00:09:02.607
Well, wait, your new book is called

00:09:02.607 --> 00:09:05.309
"Natalie Portman's
Fables" that's the name?

00:09:05.309 --> 00:09:06.203
- Yes.
- Yes and I--

00:09:06.203 --> 00:09:08.360
- Yes, I have a copy.

00:09:08.360 --> 00:09:10.770
- I know it's so great and I love,

00:09:10.770 --> 00:09:14.090
I mean, the art is so
cool it's so classic.

00:09:14.090 --> 00:09:15.340
I was looking at it during lunch

00:09:15.340 --> 00:09:18.350
and I was freaking out
over it, it's so well done.

00:09:18.350 --> 00:09:22.250
- Yeah Janna Mattia the
illustrator is so talented

00:09:22.250 --> 00:09:24.960
and I was so lucky and she
has so many like little

00:09:24.960 --> 00:09:27.100
jokes inside the art that I love

00:09:27.100 --> 00:09:29.140
that my daughter loves finding like

00:09:30.664 --> 00:09:33.360
there's a rabbit taking
a tortoise out of the hat

00:09:33.360 --> 00:09:38.360
and there's a porcupine
making balloon animals--

00:09:40.730 --> 00:09:41.740
- Oh my God!

00:09:41.740 --> 00:09:43.830
- With all the thorns and it's just

00:09:43.830 --> 00:09:47.130
there's so many sweet
details that she put in

00:09:47.130 --> 00:09:49.810
that are so fun for kids to notice.

00:09:49.810 --> 00:09:50.770
- I love that.

00:09:50.770 --> 00:09:53.310
It's like back in the day
like pop up video of MTV

00:09:53.310 --> 00:09:54.410
like did you know?

00:09:54.410 --> 00:09:56.800
And it would be like,
that's, it's amazing.

